/**
 * Front-door admission and content screening: Hosted sending checks validation, recipient suppression/consent, balance, limits, and the outbound harm screen synchronously before queueing. Failing messages return an immediate error (422 RECIPIENT_INELIGIBLE, 422 CONTENT_REJECTED_SPAM, 422 CONTENT_TOO_LARGE_TO_SCREEN, or retryable 503 CONTENT_SCREEN_UNAVAILABLE with Retry-After when no mandatory classifier is configured) and nothing is queued; during a temporary outage of both classifiers, eligible mail is accepted (202) and held unscreened until a classifier answers. Screened-text limits: at most 65,536 UTF-8 bytes per message (installation default) and 150 distinct link hosts (3,000 bytes of host names), counting link labels that name another host; over either limit, error.details[0] holds { screenedBytes, limitBytes } or { linkHosts, linkHostLimit } (plus index in a batch). Marketing ramp: on Hosted sending, a workspace still ramping up (a new sender, exempt and first-party ones included) sends kind: marketing at most its ramp step's hourly ceiling (1,000, 2,500, 5,000, 10,000, 20,000, 40,000, 75,000, 150,000, 300,000 recipients an hour), shared with its campaigns. A send that would cross it is refused with nothing queued (a batch as a whole): retryable 429 RAMP_LIMIT_REACHED, with Retry-After (seconds until more room opens) and error.details[0] holding { step, steps, ceilingPerHour, usedThisHour, fitsNow, requested, stepOpensAt }. A scheduled marketing send is refused the same way when it is scheduled if it is larger than the step's whole ceiling, or if with the marketing already scheduled within 24 hours either side of its time it is more than the step sends in 24 hours (error.details[0] adds { scheduledBacklog, dailyBudget }); one that fits is held at its time until its hour has room (it stays scheduled, scheduledAt moving to when it tries again, with a dispatch_deferred event), never rejected for the ramp. While the workspace ramps and a campaign of it is stopped by the automatic pause, the list probe or the ramp's check (LIST_QUALITY, BOUNCE_RATE_HIGH, COMPLAINT_RATE_HIGH) and not resumed or canceled, marketing sends and schedules are refused with 409 RAMP_PAUSED (not retryable; error.details[0] holds { campaignId, pauseCode }): review and resume that campaign first. For a large list, send a campaign instead: campaigns are paced automatically. Transactional mail is never ramped or paused by it. Built-in domain (Hosted): every workspace has a subdomain of Nightmail's own domain (connection.builtinDomain in GET /v1/me) that sends until the workspace verifies a domain of its own: plain transactional email sent now, without attachments, custom headers, a stored template or a Reply-To elsewhere (422 BUILTIN_DOMAIN_UNSUPPORTED, once, listing every such field in error.details[].unsupported), to the recipients the installation allows (422 BUILTIN_DOMAIN_RECIPIENT), within a per-minute and a daily recipient cap (429 BUILTIN_DOMAIN_LIMIT), screened like a new sender's mail. Once a domain of its own verifies it stops sending and answers 409 OWN_DOMAIN_VERIFIED, with the same address at that domain to use instead in error.details[0].suggestedFrom (and every verified domain in verifiedDomains). Attachments: One file is at most 8 MiB decoded when sent as base64 in JSON (POST /v1/attachments), or 25 MiB as raw bytes (POST /v1/attachments/upload, with the name URI-encoded in X-Nightmail-Filename and the type in Content-Type). One email carries at most 100 files and 25 MiB of them together, decoded. A workspace keeps at most 100 GiB of uploaded files. For anything larger, link to the file from the email. filename: the file's name: 1 to 200 characters, without control characters, / or \, and not an extension Amazon SES refuses (.exe, .bat, .js, .vbs and others). contentType: a MIME type such as application/pdf (type/subtype, optionally with ; parameters), at most 78 printable ASCII characters. disposition: attachment (the default) or inline; inline needs a contentId: 1 to 78 letters, digits, _ . @ or -, for an inline image the HTML shows as cid:<contentId>, unique within the email. Upload each file once, then list its ID in the email's attachments. A send with attachment problems is refused once, naming every problem: error.details lists each with its email (index, in a batch), attachment (its position in attachments), id, filename, code and message. Hosted senders on probation attach only standard types (images, PDF, office documents, archives, audio, video, plain text) whose name, declared type and content agree, inline only PNG, JPEG, GIF, WebP or BMP images, and never SVG.
 */
export const sendEmail = <ThrowOnError extends boolean = false>(options: Options<SendEmailData, ThrowOnError>): RequestResult<SendEmailResponses, SendEmailErrors, ThrowOnError> => (options.client ?? client).post<SendEmailResponses, SendEmailErrors, ThrowOnError>({
    security: [
        { scheme: 'bearer', type: 'http' },
        {
            in: 'cookie',
            name: 'nightmail.session_token',
            type: 'apiKey'
        },
        {
            in: 'cookie',
            name: '__Host-nightmail.session_token',
            type: 'apiKey'
        }
    ],
    url: '/v1/emails/send',
    ...options,
    headers: {
        'Content-Type': 'application/json',
        ...options.headers
    }
});

